yoyo map "dir sp" now maps the directory instead of refusing with "two paths given". It's committed as 3434e89a, and build, the full test suite, clippy and fmt all pass.

What was wrong: the shell map command built its input with quote_args_as_command, which wrapped dir sp in literal quotes. The map parser then split on whitespace and saw two paths, "dir and sp". So a directory with a space in its name couldn't be mapped at all.

Fix: following the pattern from the #1008 fixes, the shell's map command now hands each argument straight to a new handle_map_argv_status, and each argument counts as one token. The REPL's /map uses the same parsing loop, so flags, ./ stripping, the "two paths" refusal and exit codes 0/1/2 behave the same in both places. Typing /map dir sp in the REPL still means two paths. I didn't change quote_args_as_command, because other commands still depend on it.

Lookup table (step 0, in the commit message): ls, tree, du -s and find all treat "dir sp" as one path, so map now does too.

Tests:
• An end-to-end integration test failed before the fix with the exact reported error. It now exits 0 and lists main. The near-misses are unchanged: map a b still exits 2 with "two paths given", --bogus exits 2, a missing path exits 1, bare map exits 0, and --all "dir sp" works.
• A unit test checks the path arrives byte-for-byte, and that the new argument parser and the REPL's parser give the same result on the near-miss inputs.

One change to the plan: the task's fixture used a private fn main(){}, but map shows only public symbols by default, so that fixture would print the honest "no symbols" result. I used pub fn main instead; the private one does show up under --all.

Still to do in #1009: grep (interior quotes), watch, outline, docs, skill and lint still receive the quote-wrapped input. The commit message says so, and I added a matching Day 227 note to dispatch_sub.rs's entry in ARCHITECTURE.md.
